{
  "title": "Xor Streak Threshold",
  "description": "## Task\n\nA sensor logs integers. A streak is any non-empty run of consecutive readings; its signature is the bitwise XOR of all readings in it. Count the streaks whose signature is at least `k`.\n\n## Input\n\n- `xs` — the readings in order.\n- `k` — the threshold.\n\n## Output\n\nThe number of pairs `(i, j)` with `0 <= i <= j < len(xs)` such that `xs[i] ^ xs[i+1] ^ ... ^ xs[j] >= k`.\n\n## Example\n\n```python\nxor_streak_threshold([1, 2, 3], 3)  # returns 3\nxor_streak_threshold([4], 5)  # returns 0\nxor_streak_threshold([], 0)  # returns 0\n```\n\n## Constraints\n\n- 0 ≤ length of `xs` ≤ 50000\n- 0 ≤ each reading, `k` < 2^30\n- Examining every streak is too slow for the longest inputs.\n",
  "signature": "def xor_streak_threshold(xs: list[int], k: int) -> int",
  "topic": "prefix xor trie",
  "difficulty": "hard",
  "category": "math-bits",
